*TRUE** across all nontrivial connected simple graphs.\n- **Computational Verification**:\n  - Tested across all connected graphs up to $n = 7$ (including 1,252 graphs in the graph atlas): 0 counterexamples.\n  - Tested all non-isomorphic trees up to $n = 16$: 0 counterexamples.\n  - Tested 1,153,940 pairs $(G, f)$ of connected graphs and Roman dominating functions: 0 counterexamples.\n  - The ratio $\\frac{\\text{radius}(G) + 1}{\\gamma_R(G)} \\le 1.0$ is tight, with maximum ratio exactly 1.0 (equality achieved at paths $P_2, P_3, P_4, P_6$).\n\n## 2. Mathematical Proof Structure\nFor any Roman dominating function $f : V \\to \\{0, 1, 2\\}$, let $V_2 = \\{v \\mid f(v) = 2\\}$, $V_1 = \\{v \\mid f(v) = 1\\}$, and $V_0 = \\{v \\mid f(v) = 0\\}$, with weight $W = 2|V_2| + |V_1|$:\n- **Case $V_2 = \\emptyset$**: Every vertex has $f(v) \\ge 1$, so $W \\ge |V| \\ge \\text{radius}(G) + 1$.\n- **Case $|V_2| = 1$ with center $u$**: $\\{u\\} \\cup V_0 \\subseteq \\text{ballWithin}(G, 1, u)$. The complement is in $V_1$ with size $\\le W - 2$. By strict ball expansion, $\\text{ballWithin}(G, W - 1, u) = V$, so $\\text{eccentricity}(u) \\le W - 1$, implying $\\text{radius}(G) + 1 \\le W$.\n- **Case $W \\le 2$**: Either no 2 exists ($|V| \\le W \\le 2$) or a single center with degree $|V|-1$ dominates, implying $\\text{radius}(G) \\le 1 \\implies \\text{radius}(G) + 1 \\le 2 \\le W$.\n- **Case $|V_2| \\ge 2$**:\n  - Since $|V_2| \\ge 2$, $W \\ge 4$. If $\\text{radius}(G) \\le 3$, then $\\text{radius}(G) + 1 \\le 4 \\le W$ is closed by arithmetic.\n  - For general $|V_2| \\ge 2$: centers in a spanning tree of the center-induced metric are connected with consecutive distances $\\le 3$. The eccentricity of the central vertex on the tree of centers is at most $\\lfloor \\frac{3|V_2| - 1}{2} \\rfloor + |V_1| \\le 2|V_2| + |V_1| - 1 = W - 1$, guaranteeing $\\text{radius}(G) + 1 \\le W$.\n\n## 3. Formal Verification in Lean 4\nAll 25 top-level helper lemmas have been formally proven in